(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 42-year-old treated for a corneal abrasion three days ago returns with worsening pain and a white spot on the cornea. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A hyphema has developed","A corneal ulcer has developed","The abrasion is healing normally","A rust ring has formed spontaneously","The fluorescein has stained the stroma permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is why abrasions are re-examined, and why anaesthetic drops are never sent home. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 8-year-old struck by a ball has severe pain, vomiting and a heart rate of 48 whenever he tries to look up. His eye and lids look normal. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Simple concussion","Vasovagal syncope from pain","Traumatic iritis","Migraine triggered by the injury","A white-eyed blowout"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. In children entrapment can occur with no orbital soft tissue signs at all. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does the bruising in an orbital contusion stay in front of the eye rather than spreading behind it?","choices":["The septum acts as a wall","The orbital fat absorbs the blood","The periosteum is impermeable","Venous drainage carries it forward","The globe blocks posterior spread"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is what keeps it preseptal. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 19-year-old arrives with a fish hook embedded through the cornea. He is stable and in pain. Which is the most appropriate management of the hook?","choices":["Leave it in place and arrange surgical repair","Withdraw it along its entry path","Cut it flush with the cornea and cover it","Push it through and out to avoid the barb","Remove it after topical anaesthetic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removing it can extrude intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 41-year-old welder felt something strike his eye while grinding without goggles. Vision is 20/40 and the eye is quiet. Which imaging is appropriate, and which must be avoided?","choices":["Computed tomography of the orbit; avoid magnetic resonance imaging","Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbit; avoid computed tomography","Ocular ultrasound with firm probe pressure; avoid computed tomography","Plain orbital radiograph alone; avoid computed tomography","No imaging, since acuity is preserved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A magnetic field can move a metallic fragment through the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with long-standing proliferative diabetic retinopathy has a localised, concave retinal detachment. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Intravitreal antibiotics","Laser to the macula alone","Surgical repair","Treat the underlying systemic disease alone","Observation with serial imaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A traction detachment is managed surgically. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 26-year-old with hyphema has an intraocular pressure of 34 mmHg (normal 10 to 21) and no suspicion of an open globe. Which agents are used?","choices":["Beta blockers and acetazolamide","Topical corticosteroids alone","Aspirin to improve perfusion","Aminocaproic acid alone","A mydriatic to open the angle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is how a raised pressure is brought down here. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 37-year-old has crepitus in the eyelid after blowing his nose following facial trauma. Which finding is this?","choices":["A periorbital haematoma","Chemosis","Enophthalmos","Subcutaneous emphysema","Orbital cellulitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Air tracks from the sinus into the orbit through the fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 40-year-old is suspected of an orbital fracture after a blow. Which imaging is ordered?","choices":["Ocular ultrasound","No imaging unless diplopia is present","Computed tomography of the orbits and midface","Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbits","Plain facial radiographs alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the study for this fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 35-year-old has periorbital swelling and ecchymosis after a fall, with a normal globe and no haemorrhage within the orbit. Beyond supportive care, which is the most appropriate step?","choices":["Arrange urgent canthotomy","Refer for cataract assessment","Begin a corticosteroid taper","Exclude brain trauma","Start topical antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The force that bruised the orbit reaches the brain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old with an eye injury is intoxicated and gives an inconsistent account. Which is the most appropriate way to establish the mechanism?","choices":["Accept the account as given","Record the mechanism as unknown and proceed","Ask family, friends or bystanders","Rely on the examination findings alone","Wait until he is sober to take a history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The circumstance of injury still has to be established. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 22-year-old with a traumatic hyphema is admitted. Which regimen fits, and what is it aiming at?","choices":["Bed rest head-up and aminocaproic acid, to prevent a rebleed","Ambulation and warm compresses, to clear the blood faster","Aspirin, to prevent clot organisation","Topical anaesthetic and discharge, with review in a week","Immediate surgical washout in all cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every element serves that one goal. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 16-year-old was hit in the eye by a paintball. There is a layer of blood across the lower anterior chamber with a visible fluid level. Which is the most likely diagnosis, and what else must be considered?","choices":["Vitreous haemorrhage seen through the pupil","Traumatic iritis with cells in the chamber","Hyphema, and it can itself indicate an open globe","Hyphema, which rules out an open globe","Subconjunctival haemorrhage overlying the iris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is not always an isolated injury. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A metallic corneal foreign body is removed from a 45-year-old, leaving a brown ring in the stroma. Which is the most appropriate management of that ring?","choices":["Irrigate repeatedly with saline","Wipe it with a cotton-tipped applicator","Leave it, since it will resorb","Prescribe a chelating drop","Remove it with a battery-operated burr"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A rust ring from iron or copper comes out with a burr. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has double vision on looking to the side after blunt orbital trauma. Which muscle is entrapped?","choices":["Superior oblique","Medial rectus","Inferior rectus","Superior rectus","Inferior oblique"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lateral gaze diplopia points to the medial rectus and a medial wall fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with hypertension and a known intraocular tumour has subretinal fluid but no retinal break on dilated examination. Which type of detachment is this?","choices":["Posterior vitreous detachment","Exudative","Rhegmatogenous","Traction","Combined rhegmatogenous and traction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No break and no traction, with an associated tumour. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 21-year-old has a full-thickness upper lid laceration. Beyond repairing the lid, which is the most important step?","choices":["Prescribe a cycloplegic","Examine the globe underneath","Start oral corticosteroids","Arrange a visual field test","Patch the eye for 48 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About two thirds of full-thickness lid lacerations come with one. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 23-year-old has a laceration of the upper lid and a subconjunctival haemorrhage after being struck with a bottle. He is alert. Which is the most appropriate next step before any lid repair?","choices":["Assess the globe beneath","Close the lid and arrange follow-up","Instil a mydriatic to see the retina","Measure the intraocular pressure first","Discharge with oral antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two thirds of full-thickness lid lacerations have a globe injury with them. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which bones may be involved in a basilar skull fracture?","choices":["The nasal bones and the vomer","The mandible and the temporomandibular joint","The parietal bones only","Ethmoid, frontal, temporal, sphenoid or occipital","The maxilla and the zygomatic arch alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those make up the skull base. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 58-year-old myope describes a shower of floaters, flashes of light, and a curtain moving across the vision of one eye. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Exudative retinal detachment","Traction retinal detachment","Vitreous haemorrhage alone","Central retinal vein occlusion","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Flashes, floaters and a curtain, with myopia as the risk factor. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 52-year-old has subretinal fluid with no retinal break and no traction, alongside a known intraocular tumour. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Pneumatic retinopexy","Laser retinopexy around a break","Observation with no treatment","Treat the underlying condition","Scleral buckling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An exudative detachment is not primarily surgical. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Computed tomography confirms an orbital floor fracture with inferior rectus entrapment in a 30-year-old. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["List for elective repair in six weeks","Contact a facial trauma surgeon urgently","Ice, analgesia and review in 2 to 3 days","Admit for neurosurgical observation","Start antibiotics and discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An entrapped muscle can necrose if its blood supply is compromised. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 31-year-old has a corneal abrasion from a fingernail. Which treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Oral antibiotics alone","No treatment and no follow-up","A topical antibacterial","A topical antiviral","A topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and patching may ease the pain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 33-year-old has a 1 centimetre laceration running through the lower lid margin after a dog bite. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Tissue adhesive with no follow-up","Ophthalmology, because the lid margin is involved","Close it in the emergency department with fine sutures","Leave it open and review in one week","Refer to plastic surgery as an outpatient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Margin involvement is one of the explicit referral criteria. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 72-year-old with previous cataract surgery in both eyes is struck in the face by a car airbag. Why does the surgical history matter here?","choices":["It rules out an open globe","It means the pressure will read falsely high","Old incisions leave a permanent weak point","It makes a hyphema more likely than a rupture","It protects the eye from blunt force"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prior intraocular surgery lowers the force needed. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old on warfarin fell and struck his face, and is now confused. His right eye is bruised. Which is the most appropriate imaging decision?","choices":["No imaging unless vision is reduced","Magnetic resonance imaging of the head","Plain skull radiographs","Ocular ultrasound only","Computed tomography without contrast"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Both are on the list that makes imaging automatic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which detail about the mechanism most changes the level of concern in ocular trauma?","choices":["Whether the patient was wearing spectacles","The time of day the injury happened","Whether the patient drove themselves in","The patient's occupation alone","Blunt or sharp, high or low velocity"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That is what predicts the injury pattern. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of these lid lacerations can reasonably be repaired in the emergency department?","choices":["A cut through the lid margin","A cut 4 millimetres from the medial canthus","A cut associated with new ptosis","A cut through the tarsal plate","A superficial skin cut sparing the margin"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Partial-thickness lacerations meeting none of the criteria are repaired there. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 60-year-old on warfarin develops a tense, proptotic orbit with rising pressure and no history of injury. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Observation until it resorbs","Topical beta blocker alone","Immediate enucleation","Canthotomy with cantholysis","Pressure patching the eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Releasing the lateral canthal tendon and cutting its inferior branch lets the blood drain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 20-year-old with a hyphema is being discharged after admission. Which instruction matters most?","choices":["Resume contact sport as soon as the blood clears","Sleep flat to help the blood disperse","Stop the cycloplegic once the pain settles","Avoid aspirin, antiplatelets and straining","Use topical anaesthetic drops when the eye aches"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both raise the risk of a secondary haemorrhage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
